                        <p style="color: black !important;">A kitten should not be separated from its mother until it is at least twelve weeks old, and if a kitten is crossing borders, it should be at least fifteen weeks old before leaving. Therefore, it is important to stay informed about their age and verify this information. When interacting with a responsible breeder, they will have knowledge of the kitten's birthplace, parents, and behavior, and will be happy to provide you with these details.</p>


                        <p style="color: black !important;" class="py-3">Before deciding to bring the kitten home, it's important to inquire about any nervousness or health issues the kitten may have. During your visit to pick up your new pet, be attentive to any concerns raised and watch for signs of potential problems. It's recommended that kittens receive worming, flea treatment, and their initial vaccination at 9 weeks of age, followed by a second vaccination at 12 to 15 weeks. Make sure to ask about these treatments and request a copy of the records beforehand. All vaccination details are documented in an animal passport by a qualified veterinarian.</p>


